question:
Given positive integers $m$ and $n \geq m$, determine the largest number of dominoes $(1 \times 2$ or $2 \times 1$ rectangles) that can be placed on a rectangular board with $m$ rows and $2 n$ columns consisting of cells $(1 \times 1$ squares $)$ so that:



(i) each domino covers exactly two adjacent cells of the board;



(ii) no two dominoes overlap;



(iii) no two form a $2 \times 2$ square; and



(iv) the bottom row of the board is completely covered by $n$ dominoes.

response:
The bottom row can be covered by $n$ dominoes in only two ways, as shown in the figure below.

[asy]
size(2cm);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle);
draw((0,1)--(2,1));
draw((0.5,0)--(1.5,0));
draw((0.5,0)--(0.5,1));
draw((1.5,0)--(1.5,1));
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);
draw((1,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--cycle);
label("$A$", (0.5,0), S);
label("$B$", (1.5,0), S);
[/asy]

Let $N$ be the number of dominoes that can be placed on the board.  Then $N = n + k$, where $k$ is the number of dominoes that can be placed on the $m - 1$ rows above the bottom row.  We claim that $k \leq 2n - 2$, so that $N \leq 3n - 2$.  Suppose that $k \geq 2n - 1$.  Then the number of cells covered by the $k$ dominoes is $2k \geq 4n - 2$.  Since the bottom row contains $2n$ cells, at least $2n - 2$ cells in the remaining $m - 1$ rows must be covered.  These cells must be covered by the $k$ dominoes.  We can color the cells of the board in a checkerboard pattern, as shown in the figure below.

[asy]
size(4cm);
int i, j;
for(i=0; i<=3; ++i) {
  for(j=0; j<=7; ++j) {
    fill((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--cycle, i%2==j%2 ? "#ffcccc" : "white");
  }
}
[/asy]

In the figure above, we have colored the cells of the board so that each domino covers one black cell and one white cell.  Since the number of cells of each color is $2mn$, the $k$ dominoes cover an equal number of cells of each color.  Therefore, the $k$ dominoes cover at least $n - 1$ cells of each color in the $m - 1$ rows above the bottom row.  We claim that the $k$ dominoes must cover at least $n$ cells of each color in the $m - 1$ rows above the bottom row.  Suppose, for the sake of contradiction, that the $k$ dominoes cover $n - 1$ cells of one color in the $m - 1$ rows above the bottom row.  Since the $k$ dominoes cover an equal number of cells of each color, the $k$ dominoes cover $n - 1$ cells of the other color in the $m - 1$ rows above the bottom row.  Then the $k$ dominoes cover $2n - 2$ cells in the $m - 1$ rows above the bottom row.  This means that the $k$ dominoes cover at most $2$ cells in the bottom row.  Since the bottom row is covered by $n$ dominoes, at least $n - 1$ dominoes in the bottom row must cover cells of the same color.  This is impossible, so the $k$ dominoes must cover at least $n$ cells of each color in the $m - 1$ rows above the bottom row.  Thus, the $k$ dominoes cover at least $2n$ cells in the $m - 1$ rows above the bottom row.  Since the $k$ dominoes cover $2k \geq 4n - 2$ cells in total, the $k$ dominoes cover at least $2n - 2$ cells in the bottom row.  Since the bottom row is covered by $n$ dominoes, at least $n - 1$ dominoes in the bottom row must cover cells of the same color.  This is impossible, so $k \leq 2n - 2$.

Therefore, the largest number of dominoes that can be placed on the board is $3n - 2$.  We claim that this number can be achieved.  We have two cases, depending on whether $m$ is even or odd.

Case 1: $m$ is even.

We cover the bottom row with $n$ dominoes in the configuration $B$, as shown in the figure below.

