draw((1,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--cycle);
label("$A$", (0.5,0), S);
[/asy]

We then cover the remaining $m - 1$ rows with $2n - 2$ dominoes in the configuration $B$, as shown in the figure below.

[asy]
size(4cm);
int i, j;
for(i=0; i<=4; ++i) {
  for(j=0; j<=7; ++j) {
    if (i%2 == 0) {
      fill((j,i)--(j+1,i)--(j+1,i+1)--(j,i+1)--cycle, j%2 == 0 ? "#ffcccc" : "white");
      fill((j+1,i)--(j+2,i)--(j+2,i+1)--(j+1,i+1)--cycle, j%2 == 0 ? "white" : "#ffcccc");
    } else {
      fill((j,i)--(j+1,i)--(j+1,i+1)--(j,i+1)--cycle, j%2 == 0 ? "white" : "#ffcccc");
      fill((j+1,i)--(j+2,i)--(j+2,i+1)--(j+1,i+1)--cycle, j%2 == 0 ? "#ffcccc" : "white");
    }
  }
}
[/asy]

Therefore, the largest number of dominoes that can be placed on the board is $3n - 2$.
</think>

<answer>\boxed{3n-2}</answer>

