First, we claim that $E(9,5)=\infty$.

We will show that it is impossible to have all switches on at the end of any minute. We will show this by contradiction. Suppose that at some point in time all switches are on. Let $S$ be the set of switches that are on, so $|S|=9$.

At the beginning of the puzzle, all switches are off, so $S=\emptyset$. Every minute, exactly 5 switches are flipped. Thus, the number of switches that are flipped changes by 5 every minute. If we let $f(n)$ be the number of switches that are flipped after $n$ minutes, then $f(n) \equiv n \pmod{2}$.

In particular, if $|S| = 9$ then $9 \equiv f(n) \pmod{2}$, so $f(n)$ is odd. However, if $|S| = 0$ then $0 \equiv f(n) \pmod{2}$, so $f(n)$ is even. This is a contradiction, so it is impossible to have all switches on. Thus, $E(9,5)=\boxed{\infty}$.

